 :lesson/sections
 [{:section/type :accuracy-pointer,
   :section/id 18,
   :section/title "All the days",
   :section/body
   ["In Spanish to say every day you can use cada día. It is also very common to say All the days - todos los días."
    "Él tiene que trabajar todos los días. He has to work every day."
    "All My Girlfriends If you are talking about a feminine subject todo changes to toda."
    "Todas mis amigas all my (female) friends Todos mis amigos all my male friends"]}
  {:section/type :communication-multiplied,
   :section/id 20,
   :section/title "Would he like a coffee?",
   :section/body
   ["¿A él le gustaría un café?"
    "You will remember these structures to you it is pleasing / you like Le gusta to you it would be pleasing / you would like Le gustaría To talk about he or she, the structure is the same."
    "Le gusta to him/her it is pleasing / he or she likes to him/her it would be pleasing / he or she would like Le gustaría Most times it will be obvious whom the sentence is about. But other times it will be ambiguous and confusing. When it is not obvious who is the object of the sentence, Spanish speakers use a el, a ella and a usted to identify who is the object."
    "A él le gusta México. To him Mexico is pleasing. / He likes Mexico."
    "To her Mexico is pleasing. / She likes Mexico. A ella le gusta México."
    "A usted le gusta México. To you Mexico is pleasing. / You like Mexico."
    "Questions just change tone of voice To him is Mexico pleasing? / Does he like Mexico? ¿A él le gusta México?"
    "¿A ella le gusta México? To her is Mexico pleasing? / Does she like Mexico?"
    "¿A usted le gusta México? To you is Mexico pleasing? / Do you like Mexico?"
    "Negations just add no between the pronoun and the phrase A él no le gusta México. To him Mexico isn't pleasing. / He doesn't like Mexico."
    "A ella no le gusta México. To her Mexico isn't pleasing. / She doesn't like Mexico."
    "A usted no le gusta México. To you Mexico isn't pleasing. / You don't like Mexico."
    "The best way to understand is to practice. So go ahead and practice understanding in exercise 23B and then use these structures to create Spanish in Exercise 23D."]}],
 :lesson/number 23,
